## React Component Standards

> **Note:** For detailed React component standards, refer to the [React Component Standards](mdc:react-components.mdc) rule file.

### Component Architecture

Cloud Burst follows the Atomic Design methodology for component organization:

#### Atomic Design Structure

- **Atoms**: Basic UI components (buttons, inputs, icons) in `src/components/ui`
- **Molecules**: Simple combinations of atoms (form fields, cards) in `src/components/common`
- **Organisms**: Complex UI sections (navigation, forms) in `src/components/features`
- **Templates**: Page layouts in `src/components/layouts`
- **Pages**: Complete pages with data fetching in `src/app`

#### Server vs Client Components

- Use Server Components by default
- Add `'use client'` directive only when necessary for:
  - Interactive components that use React hooks
  - Components that use browser-only APIs
  - Components that use event handlers

```tsx
// Server Component (default)
export default async function ProductList() {
  const products = await fetchProducts();
  return <ProductGrid products={products} />;
}

// Client Component
'use client'
import { useState } from 'react';

export function ProductFilter({ onChange }) {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('');
  // Implementation
}
```

### Component Structure

- Organize components in directories with related files
- Co-locate tests, types, and utilities with components
- Use index files for clean exports

```
components/
  Button/
    Button.tsx
    Button.test.tsx
    Button.types.ts
    index.ts
```

### Component Patterns

- Use composition over inheritance
- Use render props for flexible components
- Extract logic to custom hooks
- Use Context API for shared state
- Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` for performance optimization

### Component Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS for styling
- Use Shadcn/ui for component library
- Follow utility-first approach
- Use CSS variables for theming

## State Management Standards

### Global State

Cloud Burst uses Zustand as the primary global state management solution:

```typescript
// Store definition
import { create } from 'zustand';

interface UserStore {
  user: User | null;
  isLoading: boolean;
  setUser: (user: User | null) => void;
  clearUser: () => void;
}

export const useUserStore = create<UserStore>((set) => ({
  user: null,
  isLoading: true,
  setUser: (user) => set({ user, isLoading: false }),
  clearUser: () => set({ user: null, isLoading: false }),
}));
```

### Local State

- Use `useState` for simple component state
- Use `useReducer` for complex component state
- Extract state logic to custom hooks

### Server State

- Use TanStack Query for server state management
- Configure with proper caching and revalidation
- Use Supabase for real-time data

```typescript
// TanStack Query example
import { useQuery } from '@tanstack/react-query';

export function useUserData(userId: string) {
  return useQuery({
    queryKey: ['user', userId],
    queryFn: () => fetchUser(userId),
    staleTime: 5 * 60 * 1000, // 5 minutes
  });
}
```

### State Persistence

- Use local storage for persistent user preferences
- Use cookies for authentication state
- Use Supabase for server-side persistence

## Accessibility Standards

Cloud Burst follows WCAG 2.1 AA standards for accessibility:

### Core Requirements

- Semantic HTML structure
- Proper ARIA attributes
- Keyboard navigation support
- Screen reader compatibility
- Color contrast compliance
- Reduced motion support
- Focus management
- Form labels and descriptions
- Image alt text
- Table headers and captions

### Implementation Guidelines

#### Semantic HTML

Use semantic HTML elements to provide meaning and structure:

```html
<!-- Good: Semantic HTML -->
<header>
  <nav>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
      <li><a href="/about">About</a></li>
    </ul>
  </nav>
</header>
<main>
  <section>
    <h1>Welcome to Cloud Burst</h1>
    <p>Event photography made simple.</p>
  </section>
</main>
<footer>
  <p>&copy; 2024 Cloud Burst</p>
</footer>

<!-- Bad: Non-semantic HTML -->
<div class="header">
  <div class="nav">
    <div class="nav-items">
      <div><a href="/">Home</a></div>
      <div><a href="/about">About</a></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
```

#### ARIA Attributes

Use ARIA attributes to enhance accessibility when HTML semantics are not sufficient:

```tsx
// Good: ARIA attributes
<button
  aria-expanded={isOpen}
  aria-controls="dropdown-menu"
  onClick={toggleMenu}
>
  Menu
</button>
<div
  id="dropdown-menu"
  role="menu"
  hidden={!isOpen}
>
  {/* Menu items */}
</div>
```

#### Keyboard Navigation

Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible:

```tsx
// Good: Keyboard navigation
function KeyboardNavExample() {
  const [selectedIndex, setSelectedIndex] = useState(0);
  const items = ['Item 1', 'Item 2', 'Item 3'];
  
  const handleKeyDown = (e: React.KeyboardEvent) => {
    if (e.key === 'ArrowDown') {
      setSelectedIndex((prev) => Math.min(prev + 1, items.length - 1));
    } else if (e.key === 'ArrowUp') {
      setSelectedIndex((prev) => Math.max(prev - 1, 0));
    }
  };
  
  return (
    <ul
      role="listbox"
      tabIndex={0}
      onKeyDown={handleKeyDown}
      aria-activedescendant={`item-${selectedIndex}`}
    >
      {items.map((item, index) => (
        <li
          key={index}
          id={`item-${index}`}
          role="option"
          aria-selected={index === selectedIndex}
          tabIndex={-1}
        >
          {item}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

#### Forms

Ensure forms are accessible:

```tsx
// Good: Accessible form
function AccessibleForm() {
  const [name, setName] = useState('');
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [error, setError] = useState<string | null>(null);
  
  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <div>
        <label htmlFor="name">Name</label>
        <input
          id="name"
          type="text"
          value={name}
          onChange={(e) => setName(e.target.value)}
          aria-required="true"
          aria-invalid={!name}
        />
      </div>
      
      <div>
        <label htmlFor="email">Email</label>
        <input
          id="email"
          type="email"
          value={email}
          onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
          aria-required="true"
          aria-invalid={error ? true : false}
          aria-describedby={error ? "email-error" : undefined}
        />
        {error && (
          <p id="email-error" className="error" aria-live="polite">
            {error}
          </p>
        )}
      </div>
      
      <button type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
  );
}
```

#### Images

Provide alt text for images:

```tsx
// Good: Accessible images
<img src="/logo.png" alt="Cloud Burst Logo" />

// Decorative image
<img src="/decorative.png" alt="" role="presentation" />

// Complex image with longer description
<figure>
  <img 
    src="/chart.png" 
    alt="User growth chart" 
    aria-describedby="chart-description" 
  />
  <figcaption id="chart-description">
    Chart showing user growth from January to December 2023, 
    with a 200% increase in Q3.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
```

#### Focus Management

Manage focus for interactive components:

```tsx
// Good: Focus management
function Modal({ isOpen, onClose, title, children }) {
  const modalRef = useRef(null);
  
  useEffect(() => {
    if (isOpen && modalRef.current) {
      // Save previous focus
      const previousFocus = document.activeElement;
      
      // Focus the modal
      modalRef.current.focus();
      
      return () => {
        // Restore focus when modal closes
        if (previousFocus) {
          (previousFocus as HTMLElement).focus();
        }
      };
    }
  }, [isOpen]);
  
  if (!isOpen) return null;
  
  return (
    <div
      ref={modalRef}
      role="dialog"
      aria-modal="true"
      aria-labelledby="modal-title"
      tabIndex={-1}
    >
      <h2 id="modal-title">{title}</h2>
      <div>{children}</div>
      <button onClick={onClose}>Close</button>
    </div>
  );
}
```

#### Motion and Animation

Respect user preferences for reduced motion:

```css
/* CSS for reduced motion */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  * {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    animation-iteration-count: 1 !important;
    transition-duration: 0.01ms !important;
    scroll-behavior: auto !important;
  }
}
```

```tsx
// React component respecting reduced motion
import { useReducedMotion } from '@/hooks/useReducedMotion';

function AnimatedComponent() {
  const prefersReducedMotion = useReducedMotion();
  
  const animationStyle = prefersReducedMotion
    ? { transition: 'none' }
    : { transition: 'transform 0.3s ease-in-out' };
  
  return (
    <div style={animationStyle}>
      {/* Component content */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Accessibility Testing

Cloud Burst uses a combination of automated and manual testing for accessibility:

#### Automated Testing

- **Axe**: Integrated into component tests
- **Jest**: Unit tests for accessibility features
- **Cypress**: Integration tests for accessibility
- **Lighthouse**: Performance and accessibility audits

```typescript
// Example of accessibility testing with Jest and Axe
import { render } from '@testing-library/react';
import { axe } from 'jest-axe';
import { Button } from './Button';

describe('Button', () => {
  it('should not have accessibility violations', async () => {
    const { container } = render(<Button>Click me</Button>);
    const results = await axe(container);
    expect(results).toHaveNoViolations();
  });
});
```

#### Manual Testing

- Keyboard navigation testing
- Screen reader testing
- High contrast mode testing
- Zoom testing
- Mobile accessibility testing

### Accessibility Patterns

#### Skip Links

Provide skip links for keyboard users:

```tsx
function SkipLink() {
  return (
    <a
      href="#main-content"
      className="sr-only focus:not-sr-only focus:absolute focus:top-4 focus:left-4 focus:z-50 focus:p-4 focus:bg-white focus:text-black"
    >
      Skip to main content
    </a>
  );
}
```

#### Focus Trap

Trap focus within modals and dialogs:

```tsx
import { useEffect } from 'react';
import FocusTrap from 'focus-trap-react';

function AccessibleModal({ isOpen, onClose, children }) {
  useEffect(() => {
    if (isOpen) {
      // Disable scrolling on body
      document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
      
      // Handle escape key
      const handleEscape = (e) => {
        if (e.key === 'Escape') onClose();
      };
      
      document.addEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);
      
      return () => {
        // Re-enable scrolling
        document.body.style.overflow = '';
        document.removeEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);
      };
    }
  }, [isOpen, onClose]);
  
  if (!isOpen) return null;
  
  return (
    <FocusTrap>
      <div
        role="dialog"
        aria-modal="true"
        className="fixed inset-0 z-50 flex items-center justify-center"
      >
        <div
          className="fixed inset-0 bg-black bg-opacity-50"
          onClick={onClose}
        />
        <div className="bg-white p-6 rounded-lg z-10">
          {children}
          <button
            onClick={onClose}
            className="mt-4 px-4 py-2 bg-gray-200 rounded"
          >
            Close
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </FocusTrap>
  );
}
```

#### Live Regions

Use live regions for dynamic content:

```tsx
function Notification({ message, type }) {
  return (
    <div
      role="status"
      aria-live={type === 'error' ? 'assertive' : 'polite'}
      className={`notification ${type}`}
    >
      {message}
    </div>
  );
}
```

